<!--
  BlockML Core — BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE diagnostic

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.diagnostic.BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/bml https://blockml.org/schemas/bml.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:diag="org.blockml.bml.diagnostic"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:relation="org.blockml.bml.relation"
  xmlns:task="org.blockml.bml.task"
  xmlns:type="org.blockml.bml.type">

  <name>BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE</name>
  <type>diag:BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE</type>
  <baseType>core:Diagnostic</baseType>
  <is>An unresolved-type diagnostic</is>

  <summary>Emitted when a value whose declared type is TypeReference (or TypeReferenceEntity)
    cannot be resolved in the current TypeRegistry — including a missing dependency library.</summary>

  <keywords>BML_UNKNOWN_TYPE, unknown type, unresolved FQN, TypeRegistry, TypeReference</keywords>

  <documentation>
    The validator keys off the declared ValueType of the field — not the property or attribute
    name and not the XML site. Whether the field is authored as type=, baseType=, abc=, or any
    other name is irrelevant. If the field's type is TypeReference or TypeReferenceEntity (or a
    subtype), the authored name is expanded to a canonical FQN and looked up in TypeRegistry.
    Failure to resolve emits this diagnostic.

    If the field's type is TypeIdentifier (or a TypeIdentifier that is not a TypeReference),
    existence is not checked — an unknown but well-formed FQN is valid. Ill-formed syntax on
    either Identifier or Reference → BML_INVALID_TYPE_REFERENCE. Unresolved MemberReference →
    BML_UNKNOWN_PROPERTY (or this diagnostic when the owning type cannot be resolved).
    Unresolved InstanceReference → BML_UNRESOLVABLE_INSTANCE_REF.

    This diagnostic is an error, but it does not mean the document is ill-formed or semantically
    wrong. It is the same class of finding as Java "cannot find symbol" / class not found: the
    name may be a typo, or a library may simply not be on the current classpath (for example a
    Maven package not included). Which of those it is remains a question of interpretation.

    Common causes: typo in FQN or xmlns prefix, missing Library blocks entry, unloaded dependency
    library, or reference to a Sub-Block type without the full parent-qualified path where required.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see type="task:DebugUnknownType" relation="Resolves">
      Step-by-step fix — namespace, Library blocks, bootstrap order.
    </see>
    <see type="type:TypeReference" relation="Defines">
      Existence-checked type name — this diagnostic applies here.
    </see>
    <see type="type:TypeIdentifier" relation="ContrastsWith">
      Syntax-only type name — unknown FQN is valid.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:LibrarySystem" relation="Prerequisite">
      How libraries register blocks in TypeRegistry.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:TypeIdentityDerivation" relation="SeeAlso">
      Type resolution and unknown-type diagnostics.
    </see>
  </references>

</block>
